# Rank 1224 / CVE-2024-37358 (GHSA-56jp-w6vw-j3jw): Apache James's IMAP
# frame reader consumed a request line one byte at a time with no cap on
# how long that line could grow. NettyImapRequestLineReader.nextChar()
# increments a per-connection `read` counter on every byte pulled off the
# Netty ChannelBuffer but never compares it against anything -- a
# pre-authentication client that keeps sending bytes without a CRLF or a
# declared IMAP literal drives the frame decoder's cumulation buffer
# arbitrarily large, since ImapRequestLineReader's tag/command/argument
# parsers all loop by repeatedly calling nextChar() until a delimiter
# appears. The fix (b2f3c06e, the 3.7.x backport of PR #2282) threads a
# maxFrameLength constructor parameter down to nextChar(), which now
# throws once `read > maxFrameLength`, and the same change shipped on the
# 3.8 line; the first tagged releases carrying it are 3.7.6 and 3.8.2.
#
# The weakness is inside the library, so the application embedding the
# IMAP server is correct code -- the report that belongs to this CVE is
# the dependency finding: a Maven pin of the carrying artifact below the
# first line-bounded release. The advisory attributes the vulnerability
# to org.apache.james.protocols:protocols-imap, but the vulnerable class
# org.apache.james.imapserver.netty.NettyImapRequestLineReader ships in
# org.apache.james:james-server-protocols-imap4 (jar contents verified on
# Maven Central at 3.7.5, 3.7.6, 3.8.1 and 3.8.2; the protocols-imap jar
# carries only the org.apache.james.imap and org.apache.james.mailbox
# packages), so the advisory row pins the carrying coordinate. The
# advisory fixes two branches (everything below 3.7.6, then 3.8.0 and
# 3.8.1) and the row carries a single min_safe of 3.8.2: a 3.7.6 pin,
# fixed on the 3.7 branch, still reports, the same single-floor residual
# every two-branch advisory has. The advisory's own CWE-770 label
# (repeated allocation without a limit) is accurate, so the row keeps it.
#
# Code-level detection stays absent on both revisions of nextChar(): the
# method's only names are read, buffer and readable, ordinary field and
# method names that also appear in any byte-counting read loop, safe or
# not, and no Java analysis fact correlates the counter that is
# incremented with a bound it is or is not compared against -- the Java
# loop-identity gap the ENGINE-GAPS "1192" entry records, with this as
# its second confirmed instance.

